1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,976
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons!

2
00:00:03,676 --> 00:00:08,547
Last time, you discovered tournaments, clubs and lessons with a teacher.

3
00:00:10,422 --> 00:00:18,197
Today, a question you often hear in France: "Shall we play a trictrac?" Jacquet, trictrac, backgammon…

4
00:00:17,897 --> 00:00:20,616
are they the same game? Let's go!

5
00:00:22,708 --> 00:00:26,738
These three games belong to the same family: the tables games.

6
00:00:26,438 --> 00:00:29,746
They have been played in Europe since Roman times.

7
00:00:30,347 --> 00:00:36,149
The folding wooden board, with its two halves and its points, is called a "tablier" in French.

8
00:00:37,025 --> 00:00:48,073
Over time, each country invented its own rules: backgammon in England, trictrac and jacquet in France, tavla in Turkey, nardy in Russia and the Caucasus.

9
00:00:51,213 --> 00:00:55,633
The equipment hardly changes. Each player has fifteen checkers.

10
00:00:56,568 --> 00:01:00,871
You roll two dice and move your checkers on a board with twenty-four points.

11
00:01:01,702 --> 00:01:08,186
So where is the difference? In how you start, which way you move, and what you must do to win.

12
00:01:07,886 --> 00:01:09,765
Let's look at each game.

13
00:01:12,681 --> 00:01:18,339
Let's start with backgammon, the most widely played version in the world, in clubs and online.

14
00:01:18,489 --> 00:01:23,781
The two players move in opposite directions, and the checkers start from four different points.

15
00:01:24,641 --> 00:01:30,692
A single checker is in danger: the opponent can hit it, and it goes back to the start, on the bar.

16
00:01:30,842 --> 00:01:35,006
And there is the doubling cube, which doubles the value of the game.

17
00:01:34,706 --> 00:01:39,779
It's the game of international tournaments and federations, like the FFBG in France.

18
00:01:41,691 --> 00:01:47,245
Now jacquet, a traditional French game, widely played in families in France and Belgium.

19
00:01:48,304 --> 00:01:55,435
First surprise: each player starts with all fifteen checkers stacked on a single point, in a corner of the board.

20
00:01:56,234 --> 00:02:02,202
Second surprise: both players go around the board in the same direction, one behind the other.

21
00:02:03,463 --> 00:02:10,294
In many families, a first checker, the courier, sets off alone as a scout before the others leave their stack.

22
00:02:12,886 --> 00:02:19,785
In jacquet, you never hit. A single checker is enough to block a point: the opponent cannot land on it.

23
00:02:20,812 --> 00:02:26,872
So the game becomes a battle of blocks: you build walls to slow down the opponent following you.

24
00:02:27,521 --> 00:02:31,046
There is no doubling cube: you simply play the game.

25
00:02:30,746 --> 00:02:35,139
As in backgammon, the first to bear off all fifteen checkers wins.

26
00:02:35,289 --> 00:02:41,343
Careful: the finer rules, like the courier or doubles, vary between regions and families.

27
00:02:41,043 --> 00:02:43,282
Agree on them before you play!

28
00:02:45,815 --> 00:02:51,795
Now trictrac. It was the great tables game of seventeenth- and eighteenth-century France.

29
00:02:52,492 --> 00:02:58,184
It was played at the king's court and in salons. But its principle is very different from the other two.

30
00:02:58,878 --> 00:03:04,510
Here, you don't win by bearing off your checkers: you score points for successful moves.

31
00:03:04,210 --> 00:03:07,236
Hitting a checker, for example, earns points.

32
00:03:07,585 --> 00:03:13,009
Points are counted with tokens and small pegs placed in the holes of the board.

33
00:03:12,709 --> 00:03:16,123
Its rules are famously complex… even for an owl!

34
00:03:20,485 --> 00:03:24,358
Today, trictrac is only played by a few enthusiasts.

35
00:03:24,701 --> 00:03:26,880
And yet its name has stayed.

36
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:32,652
Thanks to its prestige, "trictrac" came to mean the board, and then backgammon itself.

37
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:37,304
That's why a French grandfather may offer you "a little trictrac"…

38
00:03:37,004 --> 00:03:39,452
when he really wants to play backgammon!

39
00:03:41,651 --> 00:03:48,336
Let's sum up. Direction: opposite in backgammon and trictrac, the same for both players in jacquet.

40
00:03:49,906 --> 00:03:57,008
The start: fifteen checkers on a single point in jacquet and trictrac, spread over four points in backgammon.

41
00:03:57,568 --> 00:04:03,540
The goal: bear off your fifteen checkers in backgammon and jacquet, score points in trictrac.

42
00:04:04,046 --> 00:04:06,902
The doubling cube only exists in backgammon.

43
00:04:06,602 --> 00:04:11,260
And it's the only one of the three played competitively all over the world.

44
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:18,283
And there you go, you can now tell backgammon, jacquet and trictrac apart.

45
00:04:17,983 --> 00:04:24,671
If you know jacquet, backgammon will soon feel familiar: same board, same dice, same racing logic.

46
00:04:25,714 --> 00:04:33,664
To get started, play backgammon for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

47
00:04:33,364 --> 00:04:38,548
Next time, I'll tell you the history of backgammon. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

48
00:04:38,248 --> 00:04:40,313
See you soon around the board!

